  "formal_scope": "Algebraic Hessian determinant det(E^T F E), determinant squares, cyclic tournament identities, closed form and limit. The analytic bridge is not a Lean theorem.",
  "written_proof_audit": [
    {
      "obligation": "Analytic bridge",
      "argument": "Local Schur complement has nonzero u_d derivative. All first and second derivatives are explicit; differentiating Φ(u′,ψ(u′))=0 and substituting u′=iθ gives Hess f=E^T F E, including its sign."
    },
    {
      "obligation": "Algebraic reduction",
      "argument": "F=(I+Y²)(I-Y²)⁻¹ has unit row and column sums. A bordered basis gives det(E^T F E)=d det F."
    },
    {
      "obligation": "Nonvanishing and rational square",
      "argument": "Skew eigenvalues have modulus at most d-1. Both relevant determinants are squares of positive integers; conjugation and transpose identify the Gaussian determinant with an ordinary integer."
    },
    {
      "obligation": "Cyclic formula",
      "argument": "S(I+P)=I-P and the odd-cycle permutation determinant yield the exact formula at each odd order."
    },
    {
      "obligation": "Limit and expansion",
      "argument": "The normalized finite-order powers converge to e, e⁻¹ and e^i. The logarithmic expansion accounts for the stated odd coefficients."
    },
    {
      "obligation": "General spectral bounds",
      "argument": "Jensen gives the lower bound. A direct signed-permutation phase argument proves the spectral-radius input; convex maximization gives the upper bound, including r=0."
    },
    {
      "obligation": "Quantitative nondegeneracy",
      "argument": "Every eigenvalue of F lies in [a_d,1]. Congruence by E gives a_d(I+J)≼Hess f≼I+J and the stated determinant bounds."
    }
  ],
